-- title:
--   Unitary evolution $U(t) = \exp[-iHt]$
-- statement:
--   For a Hamiltonian $H$ on a finite-dimensional Hilbert space and a real time $t$, the **evolution operator** is
--   $$
--   U(t) = \exp[-iHt],
--   $$
--   the matrix exponential of $-itH$ (with $\hbar = 1$). It is the book's $U_{SA}(t)$ for the system-apparatus Hamiltonian $H$ and $U_a(t) = \exp[-iH_a t]$ for a controlled system Hamiltonian $H_a$.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The exponential is Mathlib's matrix exponential; it does not depend on a choice of norm. No Hermiticity is built in: the definitions and theorems that use $U(t)$ assume it of $H$.
-- source:
--   Bacon, Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1, §3.2, after eq. (3.1), p.31 (PDF p.48), and eq. (3.2), p.32 (PDF p.49)

import Mathlib

namespace BaconThesis.Control

/-- Bacon, *Decoherence, Control, and Symmetry in Quantum Computers*, arXiv:quant-ph/0305025v1,
§3.2, after eq. (3.1), p.31 (PDF p.48), and eq. (3.2), p.32 (PDF p.49): the unitary evolution
`U(t) = exp[−iHt]` generated by a Hamiltonian `H` (the book's `U_SA(t)` for the system-apparatus
Hamiltonian, and `U_a(t) = exp[−iH_a t]` for the controlled Hamiltonian `H_a`).

**Formalization Note.** `H` is a square complex matrix on any finite index type `ι`; the
exponential is Mathlib's matrix exponential `NormedSpace.exp` (it does not depend on a choice of
norm), applied to `(−i t) • H`. The time is any real `t`, and `ħ = 1` as in the book. No
Hermiticity is built in; the definitions and theorems that need it assume it. -/
noncomputable def evol {ι : Type} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι] (H : Matrix ι ι ℂ) (t : ℝ) :
    Matrix ι ι ℂ :=
  NormedSpace.exp ((-(Complex.I * (t : ℂ))) • H)

end BaconThesis.Control


